 "texts": [
  {
   "profile": "J1",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## What photosynthesis is\n- No rush: Photosynthesis is the process plants use to make their own food.\n- Plants take in carbon dioxide through tiny openings in their leaves.\n- Plants take in water from the soil through their roots.\n\n## Where it happens\n- Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n- Inside the cells of a leaf are small green structures called chloroplasts.\n- They contain a green pigment called chlorophyll.\n\n## How light energy is used\n- Chlorophyll absorbs light energy from the sun, mostly red and blue light.\n- Chlorophyll reflects green light, which is why leaves look green.\n- Without chlorophyll the plant could not capture the light energy it needs.\n\n## Products and why they matter\n- Oxygen is released as a by-product of photosynthesis.\n- The oxygen leaves the plant through the same tiny openings in the leaves.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen, which is one reason plants matter so much to life on Earth.\n\n## Word equation and limits\n- Word equation: carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, makes glucose plus oxygen.\n- A plant that gets too little light, water or carbon dioxide makes less glucose and grows more slowly.\n- This is why a plant kept in a dark cupboard turns pale and weak.",
    "mirror": "## What photosynthesis is\n- Photosynthesis is the process plants use to make their own food.\n- A plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny openings in its leaves.\n- A plant takes in water from the soil through its roots.\n- Using light energy from the sun, the plant turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose.\n- Glucose is a kind of sugar that is the plant's food.\n- The plant uses glucose to grow and to store energy.\n\n## Where photosynthesis happens\n- Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n- Inside leaf cells are small green structures called chloroplasts.\n- Chloroplasts contain a green pigment called chlorophyll.\n- Chlorophyll absorbs light energy from the sun, mostly red and blue light.\n- Chlorophyll reflects green light, which is why leaves look green.\n- Without chlorophyll the plant could not capture the light energy it needs.\n\n## Oxygen as a by-product\n- Oxygen is released as a by-product of photosynthesis.\n- The oxygen leaves the plant through the same tiny openings in the leaves.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen.\n- Plants matter so much to life on Earth because they provide oxygen.\n\n## Word equation for photosynthesis\n- The whole process can be written as a word equation.\n- Carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, makes glucose plus oxygen.\n\n## What happens when a plant gets too little\n- A plant that gets too little light, too little water or too little carbon dioxide makes less glucose.\n- The plant grows more slowly when it makes less glucose.\n- A plant kept in a dark cupboard turns pale and weak."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J2",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## What a two-step equation is\n- A two-step equation needs two steps to solve, such as 2x + 3 = 11.\n- The goal is to get the variable x alone on one side of the equals sign.\n- To do that we undo the operations that have been done to x, using the opposite operation.\n\n## The golden rule: balance both sides\n- The most important rule is that whatever you do to one side of the equation you must do to the other side.\n- An equation is like a balanced scale; doing the same thing to both sides keeps it balanced.\n\n## Step-by-step method\n- Step 1: undo addition or subtraction first.\n- In 2x + 3 = 11, 3 is added, so subtract 3 from both sides to get 2x = 8.\n- Step 2: undo multiplication or division next.\n- Here x is multiplied by 2, so divide both sides by 2 to get x = 4.\n\n## Check your answer\n- Always check by putting the answer back into the original equation.\n- For x = 4, 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3, which is 11.\n- The left side equals the right side, so the answer is correct.\n\n## Second example: 3x - 5 = 10\n- Add 5 to both sides to get 3x = 15.\n- Divide both sides by 3 to get x = 5.",
    "mirror": "## What a two-step equation is\n- A two-step equation needs two steps to solve, such as 2x + 3 = 11.\n- The goal is to get the variable x alone on one side of the equals sign.\n- We undo the operations done to x using the opposite operation.\n\n## Step one: undo addition or subtraction\n- In 2x + 3 = 11 the number 3 is added, so we subtract 3 from both sides.\n\n## Step two: undo multiplication or division\n- Here x is multiplied by 2, so we divide both sides by 2.\n\n## The balance rule and checking\n- Whatever you do to one side you must do to the other side to keep the equation balanced.\n- If you subtract 3 from only one side the equation is no longer true.\n- Check by putting the answer back: for x = 4, 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3 which is 11.\n- The left side equals the right side so the answer is correct.\n\n## Second example: 3x - 5 = 10\n- Add 5 to both sides to get 3x = 15.\n- Then divide both sides by 3 to get x = 5."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J4",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## The simplest greeting\n- Picture this: you walk into a room and say hola at any time with anyone.\n- Step 1: Use hola for a quick hello with friends or new people.\n- hola, which means hello. You can use it at any time of day\n\n## Time-of-day greetings\n- Next, step 2: switch to buenos días for good morning.\n- Step 3: say buenas tardes for good afternoon.\n- Step 4: say buenas noches for good evening or good night.\n- Notice that días is masculine plural while tardes and noches are feminine plural.\n- So the endings change: buenos for masculine, buenas for feminine.\n- buenos días for good morning, buenas tardes for good afternoon, and buenas noches for good evening or good night\n\n## Goodbyes\n- Then, step 5: say adiós to mean goodbye.\n- Step 6: use hasta luego to mean see you later when you expect to meet again soon.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Step 9: the usual reply is bien, gracias meaning fine, thank you.\n\n## Meeting for the first time\n- Finally, step 10: say mucho gusto to mean nice to meet you.",
    "mirror": "## The basic greeting\n- Hola means hello and works at any time of day with anyone.\n- Think of it like the start button on a video game: one press opens play with friends or strangers alike.\n\n## Time‑specific greetings\n- Buenos días means good morning; buenas tardes means good afternoon; buenas noches means good evening or good night.\n- Días is masculine plural so it takes buenos; tardes and noches are feminine plural so they take buenas.\n- Notice that días is masculine plural while tardes and noches are feminine plural, so the endings buenos and buenas change.\n\n## Saying goodbye\n- Adiós means goodbye and works in any situation.\n- Hasta luego means see you later and is friendly when you expect to meet again soon.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Use the informal cómo estás with friends, family and children.\n- Use the formal cómo está usted with older people, strangers or in polite situations.\n- The typical reply is bien, gracias, meaning fine, thank you.\n- Think of it like drawing two versions of a comic panel: one casual sketch for friends, one polished frame for a formal audience.\n\n## Meeting someone new\n- Mucho gusto means nice to meet you and is said when meeting someone for the first time.\n- Optional challenge: can you list the three time‑specific greetings and their matching adjective endings from memory?"
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J3",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Long-term causes and the spark\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- Think of it like the preset loadouts in a team shooter that push every match toward a full lobby brawl; the alliances themselves are not the fight, but they make a small skirmish escalate fast.\n- The first is militarism.\n- In the years before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies, and Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see it as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- Think of it like a basketball squad that only runs full-court press in practice so every game feels like a track meet; the constant drill makes conflict feel routine.\n- The second cause is alliances.\n- Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain, and the Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, so a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n- Think of it like comic publishers fighting for shelf space and IP rights, where every new title crowds the rack and breeds suspicion.\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans, where Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- Think of it like a basketball team where every player demands the ball for their own highlight reel, so the offense falls apart.",
    "mirror": "## Four long-term causes plus one spark\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n\n## Militarism — arms race and war planning\n- Great powers built up large armies and navies before 1914.\n- Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders came to see it as a normal way to settle disputes.\n\n## Alliances — two rival blocs\n- Europe split into the Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain) and the Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy).\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, so a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n\n## Imperialism — colonial rivalry\n- Imperialism means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n\n## Nationalism — Balkan flashpoint\n- Nationalism is strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans, where Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n\n## The spark — assassination and chain reaction\n- Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914 was the spark.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain."
   }
  }
